  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_6a5dfa57-fef9-41e5-b8cd-c8e299fa4c13" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>1. Here is an inequality: \(\frac{7x+6}2\leq3x+2\)Select two of the values that are a solution to the inequality.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(x = -3\), \(x = -2\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.1.</para>
            <para>The correct answers are \(x = -3\), \(x = -2\).
     </para>
            <para>Substitute the number 1 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 1 hour.
        \(9(1)+11 \gt 15(1)\)
        \(9(1)+11 \gt 15(1)\)
        \(20\gt 15\)
        \(20\gt 15\)
        To rent a scooter for 1 hour, it costs $20 from Company A and $15 from Company B. Company A costs more than Company B.
        To rent a scooter for 1 hour, it costs $20 from Company A and $15 from Company B. Company A costs more than Company B.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="c160b1e0-fda6-40eb-a8da-4e31848f0df5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>2. Which number line is represented by the inequality \( x ≤ 6 \)?</para>
            <para> <figure id="fig-00001"><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 6. The number line to the right of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_01.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para> <figure id="fig-00002"><media alt="A number line is shown. There is a closed circle on 6. The number line to the left of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_02.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para>  <figure id="fig-00003"><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 6. The number line to the left of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_03.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para>  <figure id="fig-00004"><media alt="A number line is shown. There is a closed circle on 6. The number line to the right of 6 is highlighted.
"><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_04.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>A.</para>
            <para>B.</para>
            <para>C.</para>
            <para>D.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! <figure id="fig-00005"><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 6. The number line to the left of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_05.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.2</para>
            <para>The correct answer is <figure id="fig-00006"><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 6. The number line to the left of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_06.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para>\(x \le 6\) means that the solution would be any number less than or equal to 6.
        The graph would have a filled in circle on the number 6, because it can be equal to 6. The arrow would point to infinity on the left of the 6, because it is less than 6.
        The graph would have a filled in circle on the number 6, because it can be equal to 6. The arrow would point to infinity on the left of the 6, because it is less than 6.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <para>For questions 3 – 4, use the following information:</para>
      <para>
        <quote id="quote-00001">Scooter rental company A charges $9 per hour plus a $11 initiation fee. Scooter rental company B charges $15 per hour.</quote>
     </para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="eadc8e80-78e7-4b3a-bf8a-ae7a81f3a6b4" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>3. A customer writes the following inequality to represent the situation: \(9h + 11 > 15h\).</para>
            <para>Which of the following is true about the given inequality?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>Company A costs more than Company B.</para>
            <para>Company A costs less than Company B.</para>
            <para>Company A costs the same as Company B.</para>
            <para>h represents the number of scooters rented.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Company A costs more than Company B.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.3.
     </para>
            <para>The correct answer is Company A costs more than Company B.</para>
            <para>Substitute the number 1 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 1 hour.
        \(9(1)+11 \gt 15(1)\)
        \(9(1)+11 \gt 15(1)\)
        \(20 \lt 5\)
        \(20 \lt 5\)
        To rent a scooter for 1 hour, it costs $20 from Company A and $15 from Company B. Company A costs more than Company B.
        To rent a scooter for 1 hour, it costs $20 from Company A and $15 from Company B. Company A costs more than Company B.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_7c8cc139-3f1c-48ef-aa50-44fc5400af42" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>4. For which number of hours does Company B cost less?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>1 hour</para>
            <para>2 hours</para>
            <para>3 hours</para>
            <para>4 hours</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 1 hour</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.3

     </para>
            <para>The correct answer is 1 hour.
     </para>
            <para>
        Substitute the number 1 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 1 hour.
        Company A: 9(1)+11=$20 
        Company A: 9(1)+11=$20 
        Company B: 15(1)=$15 
        Company B: 15(1)=$15 
        To rent a scooter for 1 hour, it costs $20 from Company A and $15 from Company B.
      
        To rent a scooter for 1 hour, it costs $20 from Company A and $15 from Company B.
     </para>
            <para>
        Substitute the number 2 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 2 hours.
        Company A: 
        Company A: 
        9(2)+11=$29
        9(2)+11=$29
        Company B: 15(2)=$30
        Company B: 15(2)=$30
        To rent a scooter for 2 hours, it costs $29 from Company A and $30 from Company B.
      
        To rent a scooter for 2 hours, it costs $29 from Company A and $30 from Company B.
     </para>
            <para>
        Substitute the number 3 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 3 hours.
        Company A: 9(3)+11=$38
        Company A: 9(3)+11=$38
        Company B: 15(3)=$45
        Company B: 15(3)=$45
        To rent a scooter for 3 hours, it costs $38 from Company A and $45 from Company B.
      
        To rent a scooter for 3 hours, it costs $38 from Company A and $45 from Company B.
     </para>
            <para>
        Substitute the number 4 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 4 hours.
        Company A: 9(4)+11=$47
        Company A: 9(4)+11=$47
        Company B: 15(4)=$60
        Company B: 15(4)=$60
        To rent a scooter for 4 hours, it costs $47 from Company A and $60 from Company B.
      
        To rent a scooter for 4 hours, it costs $47 from Company A and $60 from Company B.
     </para>
            <para>Company B costs the least for 1 hour.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_41abc371-b9ff-4743-8f77-ac06b17d1353" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>For questions 5 – 7, use the following information:</para>
            <para>
              <quote id="quote-00002">Cycle Rentals offers 20 membership points for each day that a customer rents a bike. Bikes Unlimited offers 5 membership points for each day that a customer rents a bike plus 60 points for joining the membership club.</quote>
           </para>
            <para>5. The equation \(20x = 5x + 60\) represents a possible constraint about the situation. Solve for \(x\).</para>
            <para>\(x\) = ____</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The correct answer is 4. If you rent a bike for longer than 4 days, you earn more membership points at Cycle Rentals.</para>
            <para>
        \(20x=5x+60\)
        \(15x=60\)
        \(15x=60\)
        \(x=4 \) 
        \(x=4 \) 
      
     </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 4. If you rent a bike for longer than 4 days, you earn more membership points at Cycle Rentals.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="bc34b533-547f-47a7-942e-01f28d487514" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>6. Which shop offers more membership points if you rent a bike for longer than the constraint you found in the previous question?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>Cycle Rentals</para>
            <para>Bikes Unlimited</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Cycle Rentals</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Cycle Rentals.
     </para>
            <para>
        The constraint from question 5 is \(x=4\). 
        Since a number larger than 4 is needed to determine which shop offers more membership points if you rent a bike for longer than the constraint, choose to substitute in the number 5 for \(x\) to calculate how many membership points can be earned in 5 days.
        Since a number larger than 4 is needed to determine which shop offers more membership points if you rent a bike for longer than the constraint, choose to substitute in the number 5 for \(x\) to calculate how many membership points can be earned in 5 days.
        Cycle Rentals: \(20x=20(5)=100\) membership points
        Cycle Rentals: \(20x=20(5)=100\) membership points
        Bikes Unlimited: \(5x+60=5(5)+60=25+60=85\) membership points 
        Bikes Unlimited: \(5x+60=5(5)+60=25+60=85\) membership points 
      
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_90b0f163-6e68-4323-abe1-441b5d71512c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>7. Which number line represents the solution to the inequality, \(20x > 5x + 60\), representing the situation when you earn more membership points at Cycle Rentals?</para>
            <para>
              <figure id="fig-00007">
                <media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_07.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>
              <figure id="fig-00008">
                <media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with a closed circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_08.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>
              <figure id="fig-00009">
                <media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with a closed circle on the point 4. The number line to the left of 4 is shaded.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_09.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>
              <figure id="fig-00010">
                <media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the left of 4 is shaded.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_10.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>A.</para>
            <para>B.</para>
            <para>C.</para>
            <para>D.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! <figure id="fig-00011"><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_11.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.9.2 and 2.9.3</para>
            <para>The correct answer is<figure id="fig-00012"><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_12.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para>
        Solve for \(x\).
        \(20x \gt 5x+60\) 
        \(20x \gt 5x+60\) 
        \(15x \gt 60 \)
        \(15x \gt 60 \)
        \(x \gt 4\) 
        \(x \gt 4\) 
        The graph will have an open circle at 4, since the solution can not be equal to 4, with the arrow pointing to infinity to the right because the solution is greater than 4.
      
        The graph will have an open circle at 4, since the solution can not be equal to 4, with the arrow pointing to infinity to the right because the solution is greater than 4.
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="cbd8f0f5-868b-4eb0-907d-8d32d19e0c12" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>8. Which number line represents the solution to \(5+8x&lt;3(2x+4)\)?</para>
            <para>
              <figure id="fig-00013">
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_13.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>
              <figure id="fig-00014">
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_14.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>
              <figure id="fig-00015">
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_15.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>
              <figure id="fig-00016">
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_16.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>A.</para>
            <para>B.</para>
            <para>C.</para>
            <para>D.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! <figure id="fig-00017"><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_17.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.5</para>
            <para>The correct answer is<figure id="fig-00018"><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_18.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para>
        Solve for \(x\).
        \(5+8x \lt 3(2x+4)\)
        \(5+8x \lt 3(2x+4)\)
        \(5+8x \lt 6x+12\)
        \(5+8x \lt 6x+12\)
        \(5+2x \lt 12\)
        \(5+2x \lt 12\)
        \(2x \lt 7\) 
        \(2x \lt 7\) 
        \(x \lt \frac72\) 
        \(x \lt \frac72\) 
        \(x \lt 3\frac12\)
        \(x \lt 3\frac12\)
        The graph will have an open circle at \(x \lt 3\frac12\), since the solution can not be equal to \(3 \frac12\), with the arrow pointing to infinity to the left because the solution is less than \(3\frac12\).
      
        The graph will have an open circle at \(x \lt 3\frac12\), since the solution can not be equal to \(3 \frac12\), with the arrow pointing to infinity to the left because the solution is less than \(3\frac12\).
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_865a1d9b-7090-4ea4-97a2-8957c4880e3f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>9. Find the solution set to this inequality by solving the related equation: \(2x-3>\frac{2x-5}2\)</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(x&lt;\frac12\)</para>
            <para>\(x>\frac12\)</para>
            <para>\(x\leq\frac12\)</para>
            <para>\(x\geq\frac12\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(x>\frac12\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.5</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(x>\frac12\).</para>
            <para>
        Solve for \(x\).
        \(2x−3\gt\frac{2x-5}{2}\)
        \(2x−3\gt\frac{2x-5}{2}\)
        \(4x−6\gt 2x-5\)
        \(4x−6\gt 2x-5\)
        \(2x−6\gt -5\)
        \(2x−6\gt -5\)
        \(2x\gt 1\)
        \(2x\gt 1\)
        \(x\gt\frac12 \)
      
        \(x\gt\frac12 \)
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_74ac8378-e680-406b-8f15-d884098ae579" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>10. Here is an inequality: \(\frac{-10+x}4+5\geq\frac{7x-5}3\). What value of \(x\) will produce equality (or make the two sides equal)?</para>
            <para>\(x\) = ____</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 2</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.4.
     </para>
            <para>The correct answer is 2.
     </para>
            <para>
        Solve for \(x\).
        \(\frac{-10+x}{4}+5=\frac{7x-5}{3}\)
        \(\frac{-10+x}{4}+5=\frac{7x-5}{3}\)
        \(12(\frac{-10+x}{4}+5=\frac{7x-5}{3})\) 
        \(12(\frac{-10+x}{4}+5=\frac{7x-5}{3})\) 
        \(3(-10+x)+(12)(5)=4(7x-5)\)
      
        \(3(-10+x)+(12)(5)=4(7x-5)\)
     </para>
            <para>
        \(\begin{align*}
        -30+3x+60&amp;=28x-20 \\
        3x+30&amp;=28x-20\\
        3x+50&amp;=28x\\
        50&amp;=25x\\
        2&amp;=x\\
        \end{align*}\)
        The solution 2 will produce equality. It will make both sides equal.
      
        The solution 2 will produce equality. It will make both sides equal.
     </para>
            <para>
        \(\frac{-10+2}{4}+5\)
        \(\frac{-8}{4}+5\)
        \(\frac{-8}{4}+5\)
        \((-2+5)\)
        \((-2+5)\)
        3
      
        3
     </para>
            <para>
        \(\frac{(7)(2)-5}{3}\)
        \(\frac{14-5}{3}\)
        \(\frac{14-5}{3}\)
        \(\frac{9}{3}\)
        \(\frac{9}{3}\)
        3
      
        3
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
